messages:
Maria Chen: quick follow-up to the kernel plugin/attn optimization RFC. found a tricky case when we fuse dequant across requests that cross kv-cache shard boundaries. TL;DR: fusion must be aware of 'stitch points' or we risk reading stale kv offsets. :thinking:
Kevin Patel: ugh. do you have a repro? is this only for variable-length requests or also for batched fixed-length?
Maria Chen: repro attached in a gist (not included here). happens when a request A continues a stream from B and the runtime chooses a fused dequant kernel that assumes contiguous kv layout. batching + shard packing causes offsets to be non-monotonic.
Sara Linton: so kernel plugin needs a preflight step that normalizes per-request kv metadata before fusing? like a small callback that can adjust pointers or construct remap tables?
Kevin Patel: yes. and we need that callback to be cheap. can't copy full kv for every request. think: remap index array + scatter/gather in the kernel.
Maria Chen: exactly. my proposal: add a KernelPlugin.h hook: prepare_stitch(const KvMeta& meta, StitchPlan* out) that runs in O(num_shards) and emits either "no-op" or a small remap table.
David O'Neil: +1, but we also need to consider memory fragmentation on GPU. remap table + indirection makes memory access less coalesced. perf impact might be bigger than the copy.
Nate B: could we prefer fallback non-fused path if the predicted extra DRAM cost is > threshold? i.e. heuristic: if remap sparsity > 0.2 then don't fuse.
Kevin Patel: love that. makes rollout safer. add measurable metric: remap_sparsity and remap_bytes. we can gate in runtime flags and report to console.
Sara Linton: sample pseudocode for kernel selection (rough):
```
// called per-request in runtime dispatch
auto plan = plugin.prepare_stitch(kv_meta);
if (plan.type == NOOP && kernel.supports_fused_dequant) {
  select(fused_dequant_kernel);
} else if (plan.sparsity < CONFIG.FUSE_SPARSE_THRESHOLD) {
  select(fused_dequant_kernel_with_remap);
} else {
  select(dequant_then_attn_kernel); // safer, more sequential memory access
}
```
Maria Chen: also need to record a tiny telemetry event when we choose fallback so we can tune the threshold: {kernel, plan.sparsity, request_seq_len, batch_size}.
David O'Neil: plus we must ensure ABI stability: the StitchPlan must be a versioned struct so older kernels don't crash. maybe encode it as a small TLV blob the kernel can ignore if unknown.
Nate B: for canary, we should enable fused_dequant only for requests with single-shard kv and >64 token sequences. low-latency short seqs probably won't benefit.
Sara Linton: question: does this interact with quant format heterogeneity? e.g., fp8 vs int8 mixed layers.
Maria Chen: yes — plugin should also include per-layer quant-format hints so kernels can pick specialized paths. plan can carry a small bitmap of layer formats.
Kevin Patel: ok, action items: 1) Sara to sketch KernelPlugin.h with prepare_stitch + TLV plan, 2) Maria to add telemetry & profiler counters for remap_sparsity, 3) David to run memory-coalescing microbench on fused_dequant_with_remap prototype, 4) Nate to define canary gating rules.
Sara Linton: i'll drop a PR with header sketch and a tiny test.
ops-bot: deploy-check: patchset 4821 queued for eng-runtime/test, CI: unit(✅) integration(⏳)
Kevin Patel: thanks. if CI passes we can run a perf CI with a few quant models. :eyes:
Maria Chen: cheers. we should record this as a follow-up to the RFC and include the StitchPlan TLV docs. will add to the RFC thread later. :shipit:
